In particular, with $H_J(S)=\Pi_J-S^\dagger(\Pi_J)$ and
$\mathcal J_{15}=\{J\subseteq\mathsf S:1\le|J|\le2\}$, the supplied
first-read identity and its stationary consequence are

$$
\begin{aligned}
e(T)&=\sup_{t\ge0}\max_{J\subseteq\mathsf S}
\|Q_J(t)-\widehat T^\dagger(Q_J(t))\|_{\rm op},\\
Q_J(t)-S^\dagger(Q_J(t))&=O(t)^TH_J(S)O(t),\\
e(S)&=\max_{J\in\mathcal J_{15}}\|H_J(S)\|_{\rm op}
\quad\text{if }S\text{ is stationary}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{1.8}
$$

The notation $e(S)$ transports a frame channel back by $D$.
Complementation uses TP, namely $S^\dagger(I)=I$. The fifteen events
are the five singletons and the ten pairs
$\{N,2\},\{N,3\},\{N,5\},\{N,25\},\{2,3\},\{2,5\},
\{2,25\},\{3,5\},\{3,25\},\{5,25\}$.
After first rank-one outcome $s$, both conditional states reset to
$\Pi_s$; their common suffix is a probability kernel, including on
zero-probability branches. Hence every finite adaptive suffix and
permitted source-independent random control obeys the first-read bound.
These ingredients retain all complex inputs and all actual histories.

